    "turn_idx": 78,
    "turn_text": "Senator Reid. Which would, because he's ex officio on it. And it would likely include having all senators and members hear about it in a classified session. So there's the issue of what's classified from a national security perspective. Then there's the issue of where Congress shouldn't go into an ongoing or closed law enforcement investigation. And there's Grand Jury Rule 6E, which there are several ways around and where the House Judiciary Committee, as the Forum for Impeachment, has special privileges. So I would hate to see them overdo their Grand Jury Secrecy Defense. There is Executive Privilege, which they grossly overstate in this administration. I would hate to see them use executive privilege as a defense. They're early hints that they won't. And then there's the trickiest one, which is what you were talking about earlier, Tim. When you don't charge somebody, it's a prosecutor's job just to shut up. You don't get to say, as Jim Comey did for Hillary Clinton, we're not charging her. But boy, look at all this bad stuff I can say about her. That was a violation of a baseline Department of Justice policy. And the question, how they apply that in these circumstances is going to be open to a lot of examination and discussion.",
